Below is a description of the issue and the root‐cause followed by three small *SEARCH/REPLACE* patches in `src/ol/renderer/canvas/VectorLayer.js` that will dynamically expand the canvas to the rotated bounding box and avoid the cropping:

1. **What’s happening?**  
   The vector layer’s `<canvas>` is created only as large as the unrotated, axis-aligned view extent (width × height).  When you draw rotated features into it (the rotation is baked into the canvas `2d` draw transform), parts of the rotated geometry stick outside the original canvas rectangle and get clipped.

2. **The fix**  
   Once the view is rotated, compute the axis-aligned bounding‐box of the rotated rectangle:
     • `rotatedWidth  = |w·cosθ| + |h·sinθ|`  
     • `rotatedHeight = |w·sinθ| + |h·cosθ|`  
   Use those as your canvas dimensions (and in your CSS pixel‐to‐screen transform) instead of the unrotated width/height.

3. **Patches**  
   Apply these three *SEARCH/REPLACE* edits in `src/ol/renderer/canvas/VectorLayer.js`:

